I refactored the code to adopt typescript and converted three files to typescript.

It's actually not so difficult to learn and most js code will be easy to transform to typescript.
Code that contains `jsx` will have to be renamed `tsx`, any other file that contains only functions without jsx is just `ts`.

Most react components are function components which is the `FC` type importet from React in typescript.

A normal component would be refactored from 

```
export default function Layout({ title, keywords, description, children }) {
```
to 
```
const Layout: FC<{
  title: string;
  keywords: string;
  description: string;
  children: React.ReactNode;
}> = ({ title, keywords, description, children }) => {
```

this way you declare the types of the props. There's a very good udemy course on typescript that I'd recommend you to do. The greatest advantage of doing this for me is that you have the documentation in the code and you always know what a function expects and what it returns.
